@startuml "Bridge - Shape (Pattern) - Class Diagram"
skinparam linetype polyline
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor<<spacer>> transparent
  BorderColor<<spacer>> transparent
  FontColor<<spacer>> transparent
}
hide <<spacer>> stereotype
hide <<spacer>> circle
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
hide empty members
title Bridge — primitives on one side, composition on the other


interface IShape <<Abstraction>> {
  + Draw() : void
  + Erase() : void
  + SetDrawer(drawer : IShapeDrawer) : void
}

abstract class AbstractShape {
  + Name : string
  # Drawer : IShapeDrawer
}

class Circle <<RefinedAbstraction>>
class Rectangle <<RefinedAbstraction>>
class Triangle <<RefinedAbstraction>>

interface IShapeDrawer <<Implementor>> {
  + DrawLine(x1, y1, x2, y2) : void
  + DrawArc(cx, cy, r, start, sweep) : void
  + Clear(x, y, w, h) : void
}

abstract class AbstractShapeDrawer {
  + Name : string
  + Calls : IReadOnlyList<string>
  + ResetCalls() : void
}

class MacOSDrawer <<ConcreteImplementor>>
class XWindowsDrawer <<ConcreteImplementor>>

IShape <|.. AbstractShape
AbstractShape <|-- Circle
AbstractShape <|-- Rectangle
AbstractShape <|-- Triangle
IShapeDrawer <|.. AbstractShapeDrawer
AbstractShapeDrawer <|-- MacOSDrawer
AbstractShapeDrawer <|-- XWindowsDrawer

note right of IShapeDrawer
  <b>Not one method per shape.</b>
  DrawCircle() here would force every
  drawer to grow when a shape is added,
  and the two hierarchies would stop
  being independent — the only thing
  Bridge exists to buy.

  GoF p. 154: "the Implementor interface
  provides only primitive operations, and
  Abstraction defines higher-level
  operations based on these primitives."
end note

note bottom of XWindowsDrawer
  Has <b>no arc call</b>, so DrawArc builds one
  from 16 line segments — GoF's Presentation
  Manager detail (p. 157).
  The same Circle: 1 call on MacOS, 16 here.
end note

note bottom of Triangle
  Added <b>after</b> both drawers were written.
  Neither drawer changed.
end note

note as N1
  <b>The call journal is not on the implementor.</b>
  Calls sits on the abstract drawer: it is how the
  tests watch, not something a shape may ask for.
  A primitive added for a test's benefit is the
  first step towards an implementor that mirrors
  its abstraction.
end note

AbstractShape -[hidden]- Rectangle
AbstractShape -[hidden]- Rectangle
Circle -[hidden]right- Rectangle
Rectangle -[hidden]right- Triangle
class " " as Spacer1 <<spacer>>
AbstractShapeDrawer -[hidden]- Spacer1
AbstractShapeDrawer -[hidden]- Spacer1
MacOSDrawer -[hidden]right- Spacer1
Spacer1 -[hidden]right- XWindowsDrawer
AbstractShape o-[norank]-> "1" IShapeDrawer : Drawer
N1 .[norank]. AbstractShapeDrawer
@enduml
